(Chapter 10: Systems, Sanity, and Scaling Up) Tj
0.720 0.470 0.100 rg
/F2 11.00 Tf
1 0 0 1 74.00 638.50 Tm
(The Business That Ate Her Weekends) Tj
0.120 0.120 0.120 rg
/F1 10.50 Tf
1 0 0 1 74.00 623.00 Tm
(Nadia's cake business worked - too well. Orders poured in, and soon she was baking at midnight,) Tj
/F1 10.50 Tf
1 0 0 1 74.00 607.50 Tm
(answering messages at dawn, and hadn't taken a day off in months. Success was quietly burning) Tj
/F1 10.50 Tf
1 0 0 1 74.00 592.00 Tm
(her out. The fix wasn't more hustle; it was systems. She wrote a simple order form so she stopped) Tj
/F1 10.50 Tf
1 0 0 1 74.00 576.50 Tm
(re-explaining everything, set fixed "kitchen hours" and order cut-off days, raised her prices to) Tj
/F1 10.50 Tf
1 0 0 1 74.00 561.00 Tm
(match demand, and eventually trained a part-time helper for busy weeks. Her income kept) Tj
/F1 10.50 Tf
1 0 0 1 74.00 545.50 Tm
(climbing - but now it climbed without eating her whole life. She'd stopped being a frantic baker and) Tj
/F1 10.50 Tf
1 0 0 1 74.00 530.00 Tm
(started being the owner of a bakery. That shift, she said, was the real graduation.) Tj
0.000 0.000 0.000 rg
/F1 11.00 Tf
1 0 0 1 60.00 499.50 Tm
(If the earlier chapters were about starting, this one is about not letting success destroy you. The dirty) Tj
/F1 11.00 Tf
1 0 0 1 60.00 483.00 Tm
(secret of a thriving home business is that it can consume your whole life if you let it. Scaling isn't just) Tj
/F1 11.00 Tf
1 0 0 1 60.00 466.50 Tm
("more" - it's more, sustainably.) Tj
/F1 11.00 Tf
1 0 0 1 60.00 444.00 Tm
(Four levers let you grow without burning out:) Tj
/F1 11.00 Tf
1 0 0 1 60.00 421.50 Tm
(1. Build simple systems. Anything you do repeatedly should become a repeatable process: an order) Tj
/F1 11.00 Tf
1 0 0 1 60.00 405.00 Tm
(form, a standard reply to common questions, a checklist for delivering your service, templates for) Tj
/F1 11.00 Tf
1 0 0 1 60.00 388.50 Tm
(invoices. Systems replace re-inventing the wheel with just... turning it. They save your time and your) Tj
/F1 11.00 Tf
1 0 0 1 60.00 372.00 Tm
(sanity.) Tj
/F1 11.00 Tf
1 0 0 1 60.00 349.50 Tm
(2. Protect your boundaries. Set working hours, order cut-offs, and days off - and hold them. "Always) Tj
/F1 11.00 Tf
1 0 0 1 60.00 333.00 Tm
(on" is not a growth strategy; it's a countdown to burnout. Rested owners make better decisions than) Tj
/F1 11.00 Tf
1 0 0 1 60.00 316.50 Tm
(exhausted ones.) Tj
/F1 11.00 Tf
1 0 0 1 60.00 294.00 Tm
(3. Raise prices as demand grows. When you have more work than time, price is your pressure valve.) Tj
/F1 11.00 Tf
1 0 0 1 60.00 277.50 Tm
(Charging more for the same hours means you can earn the same \(or more\) while working less - and) Tj
/F1 11.00 Tf
1 0 0 1 60.00 261.00 Tm
(it naturally filters for the clients you most want.) Tj
/F1 11.00 Tf
1 0 0 1 60.00 238.50 Tm
(4. Get help when it's time. You can't do everything forever. When demand outpaces you, delegate: a) Tj
/F1 11.00 Tf
1 0 0 1 60.00 222.00 Tm
(part-time helper, a contractor, or automation for repetitive tasks. Handing off work is how you) Tj
/F1 11.00 Tf
1 0 0 1 60.00 205.50 Tm
(graduate from doing the job to owning the business.) Tj
/F1 11.00 Tf
1 0 0 1 60.00 183.00 Tm
(That's the real destination of this book - not just extra cash, but a small, sturdy business that runs on) Tj
/F1 11.00 Tf
1 0 0 1 60.00 166.50 Tm
(systems instead of adrenaline, and that serves your life instead of swallowing it.) Tj
0.720 0.470 0.100 rg
/F2 11.00 Tf
1 0 0 1 74.00 130.00 Tm
(Illustration Placeholder - "Frantic Worker vs. Calm Owner") Tj
